{{-- App and auth chrome. Layout group 'default'.

    A FRAGMENT, not a document. stx synthesises the doctype, the html/head/body
    elements and their contents from config/ui.ts `app.head` merged with each
    page's useSeoMeta() (process.js:205-233 → document-shell.js). Writing a
    document here would make hasDocumentShell() true (document-shell.js:64),
    turn ensureDocumentShell() into a no-op, and silently drop every page's
    title, description and og tags on the floor.

    This layout owns the single router container, and that is load-bearing
    rather than cosmetic. config/ui.ts pins `router.container: 'main'`;
    getContainer() (stx-router client.js:193-195) returns the FIRST match, and
    when it returns null shouldIntercept bails at client.js:796 and swap()
    falls back to a full location assignment at client.js:395 — every link
    becomes a full document load and the SPA router is inert. That was this
    app's actual state until recently: the layout declared no container and
    neither did any app page, so interceptAllLinks had nothing to swap into.

    Pages MUST NOT declare their own container element. Two of them make the
    swap target ambiguous in both directions: the server slices the fragment
    between the first opening tag and the last closing one
    (serve.js:10148-10166), and the client re-injects nav, container and footer
    inside it, duplicating the chrome. Use a div for page-level wrappers.

    Comments here use the stx form, and they describe tag names in prose rather
    than spelling them out. Both rules are load-bearing: an HTML comment ships
    to the browser verbatim and hasDocumentShell's html test is unanchored, so
    the literal word in a comment disables the document shell; and the
    orphan-style extraction pass will lift a literal style tag out of the middle
    of a comment, splitting its delimiters apart so the whole comment renders as
    visible page text. See resources/partials/SiteNav.stx.

    No footer yield and no scripts stack: both were declared here and filled by
    no page anywhere in the repo. An unfilled yield resolves to '' and an
    unfilled stack renders '' — silently, with no warning — so they read as
    structure while doing nothing. Add a slot when a page pushes to it, not
    before. --}}
<main>
@yield('content')
</main>
